/*!
    \page profiler-analyze-data.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html
    \nextpage creator-qml-performance-monitor.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-profile
    \ingroup profiler-analyze-data

    \title Analyze collected data

    The \uicontrol Timeline view displays a graphical representation of the data
    and a condensed view of all recorded events.

    The timeline shows the following kinds of events, depending on the profiling
    backend:

    \list
        \li CPU usage per thread
        \li QML and JavaScript execution
        \li Trace events generated in Chrome Trace Format (CTF)
    \endlist

    \image {qtcreator-performance-analyzer-timeline.webp} {Timeline view}

    \section1 Event categories

    Each category in the timeline describes an event.

    The thread that the application runs on is labeled with the name of the
    application, where the trace has one, rather than with the name that all
    Qt applications give that thread. Move the cursor over a label to see the
    process and the thread it stands for.

    Move the cursor on an event on a row to see how long it takes and which
    function in the source it represents or where in the source it is being
    called.

    To display the information only when an event is selected, turn off
     \uicontrol {View Event Information on Mouseover}.

    \section1

    A trace that lists its events under more than one tracepoint provider, as a
    Qt application traced with \c {-trace ctf} lists them under the Qt modules
    they come from (\c qtcore, \c qtgui, and so on), also has a
    \uicontrol Providers button. Its menu lists the providers of the trace,
    with the ones that the timeline and the statistics show selected. All of
    them are selected for a trace that you have just opened or reloaded.

    Clear a provider to leave its events out. The profiler reads the trace again,
    so they are gone from the timeline and the statistics until you select the
    provider again. The last selected provider cannot be cleared, because that
    would leave nothing to look at. Events that name no provider, such as the
    events of a kernel trace recorded beside a Qt one, are always shown.

    The threads that you selected in \uicontrol {Restrict to Threads} stay
    selected. If the events of a selected thread all came from a provider that
    you clear, the profiler shows all the threads until you select that provider
    again.

    The part of the timeline that you zoomed into stays in view. If the trace
    that the profiler reads has no events there, because the providers that recorded
    them are not selected, it shows the whole trace instead.

    \section1 Outline

    The outline summarizes the period for which data was collected. Drag
    the zoom range or select the outline to move on the outline.

    You can also move between events by selecting \uicontrol {Jump to Previous Event}
    and \uicontrol {Jump to Next Event}.

    \section1 Zoom slider

    Select \uicontrol {Show Zoom Slider} to open a slider that sets the zoom
    level.

    You can also drag the zoom handles.

    To reset the default zoom level, right-click the timeline to open the
    context menu, and select \uicontrol {Reset Zoom}.

    Select the time ruler to add vertical orientation lines to the timeline.

    \if defined(qtcreator)
    \sa {Profile}{How to: Profile}, {Profilers}, {Profiling applications},
    {Performance Analyzer}

    \endif
*/
